"Internal Error ()\nciteturn57view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/deliver/fulltext/ijsem/26/2/ijs-26-2-238.pdf?itemId=/content/journal/ijsem/10.1099/00207713-26-2-238&mimeType=pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/deliver/fulltext/ijsem/26/2/ijs-26-2-238.pdf?itemId=%2Fcontent%2Fjournal%2Fijsem%2F10.1099%2F00207713-26-2-238&mimeType=pdf is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmendation of Bacteroidaceae and Butyrivibrio and Descriptions of Desulfomonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium, Clostridium, and Ruminococcus (https://scispace.com/pdf/emendation-of-bacteroidaceae-and-butyrivibrio-and-3spebb4661.pdf)\nciteturn57view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://scispace.com/pdf/emendation-of-bacteroidaceae-and-butyrivibrio-and-3spebb4661.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1449\nL0@P0: INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF SYSTEMATIC BACTERIOLOGY, Apr. zyxwvut\nL1@P0: 1976, p. 238-252\nL2@P0: Copyright zyxwvutsrq0 1976 International Association of Microbiological Societies Printed\nL3@P0: Vol. zyx\nL4@P0: in\nL5@P0: 26,\nL6@P0: U.S.A.\nL7@P0: No. 2 zy\nL8@P0: Emendation of Bacteroidaceae zyxwvand B utyrivibrio and\nL9@P0: Descriptions of Desulfornonas gen. nov. and Ten New Species\nL10@P0: in the Genera Desulfomonas, Butyrivibrio, Eubacterium,\nL11@P0: CZostridium, and Ruminococcus\nL12@P0: W. E. C. MOORE, J. L. JOHNSON, zyxwvAND L. V. HOLDEMAN\nL13@P0: Anaerobe Laboratory, Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University, Blacksburg, Virginia 24061\nL14@P0: The family Bacteroidaceae is emended to include bacteria that are monotri\u0002chous or lophotrichous. The genus Butyrivibrio is emended to include lophotri\u0002chous bacteria. Desulfomonas, a new genus of nonmotile, nonsporeforming,\nL15@P0: gram-negative, anaerobic, sulfate-reducing rods, and 10 new species of anaer\u0002obes, Desulfomonas pigra, Butyrivibrio crossotus, Ruminococcus gnavus, R.\nL16@P0: lactaris, R. obeum, Eubacterium dolichum, E. hadrum, E. ramulus, E. sir\u0002aeum, and Clostridium leptum, are described. These species have been isolated\nL17@P0: from human intestinal contents or from human feces. The type strains of these\nL18@P0: species are ATCC zyxwvuts\nL19@P0: 29098, 29175, 29149, 29176, 29174, 29143, 29173, 29099, 29066,\nL20@P0: and 29065, respectively.\nL21@P0: During studies of the bacteria from the feces\nL22@P0: and the contents of the gastrointestinal tracts\nL23@P0: of humans, cultures of several previously un\u0002described species that are predominant among\nL24@P0: the bacteria in the intestines of some individ\u0002uals were isolated. Species for which we have\nL25@P0: 10 or more isolates from six or more individuals\nL26@P0: are described below. These isolates were from\nL27@P0: studies of 177 specimens from 90 individuals.\nL28@P0: Discussion of the incidence of most of these\nL29@P0: species, as compared with other kinds of orga\u0002nisms of the intestinal flora, is published else\u0002where (3, 9).\nL30@P0: MATERIALS AND METHODS\nL31@P0: The organisms described in this report were iso\u0002lated and characterized according to anaerobic-tube\nL32@P0: culture methods and procedures published previ\u0002ously (4, 5, 9, 11, 12). Single colonies were picked\nL33@P0: from rumen fluid-glucose-cellobiose agar (RGCA)\nL34@P0: roll tubes, inoculated with dilutions of feces or\nL35@P0: intestinal contents, after incubation for 5 days\nL36@P0: under oxygen-free carbon dioxide. After one or more\nL37@P0: streaking and picking series, to verify a pure cul\u0002ture, fermentative and biochemical characteristics\nL38@P0: in prereduced media zyxwvuts(5) were determined.\nL39@P0: To determine hemolytic activity, cultures were\nL40@P0: streaked on freshly prepared blood agar plates\nL41@P0: (BAP) of brain heart infusion agar supplemented\nL42@P0: (per 100 ml) with 0.5 g of yeast extract, 0.1 p1 of\nL43@P0: vitamin K1, 0.5 mg of hemin (BHIA-S), and 5.0 ml of\nL44@P0: defibrinated sheep blood. Inoculated BAP were in\u0002cubated 40 to zyxwvuts\nL45@P0: 48 h in a vented GasPak (BBL) anaer\u0002obe jar with a gaseous atmosphere of 90% H,-10%\nL46@P0: CO,. Reactions on egg yolk agar (EYA) medium (5)\nL47@P0: were determined after similar incubation.\nL48@P0: The moles percent guanine plus cytosine (G+C)\nL49@P0: contents of the deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) prepa\u0002rations were determined by the thermal melting\nL50@P0: point (T,) method (8) by using an automatic record\u0002ing spectrophotometer (Gilford Instrument Labora\u0002tories). DNA fromEscherichia coli B was included in\nL51@P0: each set of analyses as a standard.\nL52@P0: RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\nL53@P0: Members of the following new taxa were iso\u0002lated fkom the intestinal contents or feces of\nL54@P0: humans.\nL55@P0: Desulfomonas gem nov. and D. pigra sp.\nL56@P0: nov. Desulfomonus gen. nov. (De.suZ.fo.mo'nas.\nL57@P0: L. pref. de from; L. n. sulfur sulfur; Gr. fem. n.\nL58@P0: moms unit; M. L. fem. n. Desulfomonas a cell\nL59@P0: that reduces sulfir compounds). This genus in\u0002cludes the obligately anaerobic, straight, non\u0002motile, gram-negative, nonsporeforming, sul\u0002fate-reducing rods. The metabolic activity, uti\u0002lization of pyruvate to produce acetate, reduc\u0002tion of sulfate, presence of cytochrome, and\nL60@P0: relatively high moles percent G+C content of\nL61@P0: the DNA, indicate that the genus is probably\nL62@P0: phylogenetically related to Desulfovibrio and\nL63@P0: Desulfotomaculum. However, those genera are\nL64@P0: classified according to morphological proper\u0002ties (cell shape and polar flagella, or produc\u0002tion of spores) not exhibited by the presently\nL65@P0: described organisms. Therefore, we place the\nL66@P0: genus Desulfomonas in the family Bacteroi\u0002daceae pending more information that might\nL67@P0: relate the three morphologically distinct, sul\u0002fate-reducing genera in a single higher taxon,\nL68@P0: possibly at the family level.\nL69@P0: DesuZfomonas pigra sp. nov. (L. adj. pig'ra\nL70@P0-1: 238 VOL. 26, zyxwvutsrqp\nL71@P1: 1976 EMENDATION OF zyxwBACTEROIDACEAE 239\nL72@P1: lazy [referring to the limited substrate utiliza\u0002tion of the species]); type species of the genus.\nL73@P1: Obligately anaerobic, nonsporeforming, non\u0002motile, gram-negative rods that are straight to\nL74@P1: slightly curved and have rounded ends (Fig. 1).\nL75@P1: From PY-glucose (5) broth cultures, cells were\nL76@P1: about 0.8 to 1.0 pm wide and 2.5 to 10.0 pm\nL77@P1: long.\nL78@P1: Colonies. zyxwvutsrqAfter incubation for 5 days in\nL79@P1: RGCA roll tubes, subsurface colonies were 0.5\nL80@P1: mm in diameter, lenticular, and translucent to\nL81@P1: opaque, sometimes with a dark halo that might\nL82@P1: have been produced by H,S. Surface colonies on\nL83@P1: BHIA-S roll streaks on anaerobically incubated\nL84@P1: BAP were 1 to 2 mm in diameter, circular to\nL85@P1: slightly irregular, low convex or umbonate, and\nL86@P1: translucent. There was no reaction on EYA and\nL87@P1: no hemolytic activity.\nL88@P1: Cultural characteristics. Most broth cultures\nL89@P1: showed moderate growth (2+) with turbidity\nL90@P1: and a small amount of smooth sediment.\nL91@P1: Growth was greatly enhanced zyxwvu\nL92@P1: (4 +) zyxwvutsin PY-pyru\u0002vate broth, and pyruvate seemed to serve as an\nL93@P1: energy source. The best growth was obtained at\nL94@P1: 37 C, although some strains would grow at 30\nL95@P1: and 45 C.\nL96@P1: Biochemical reactions. Characteristics of 11\nL97@P1: isolates of this species are given in Table 1. In\nL98@P1: addition, the type strain and five other strains\nL99@P1: tested did not ferment adonitol, galactose, glyc\u0002erol, inulin, sorbose, or dextrin. None of the six\nL100@P1: strains produced ammonia in PY or chopped\u0002meat (CM) broth cultures. However, the type\nL101@P1: strain and two other strains tested produced\nL102@P1: ammonia from PY-urea broth cultures, and the\nL103@P1: pH was increased 0.3 to 0.7 of a pH unit. These\nL104@P1: three tested strains did not produce oxidase.\nL105@P1: None of the 11 strains reduced nitrate.\nL106@P1: The type strain was tested for metabolic ac\u0002tivity in several media. Little or no growth was\nL107@P1: produced in the absence of peptone in glucose\u0002mineral salts-biotin medium (5) reduced with\nL108@P1: cysteine, nor was H,S or H, produced. In pep\u0002tone-Trypticase-yeast extract medium with or\nL109@P1: without glucose, growth was poor, about 0.3%\nL110@P1: H, was produced in the head space gas, and\nL111@P1: small amounts of acetic and succinic acids and\nL112@P1: large amounts of H,S (as evidenced by immedi\u0002ate solid black color with the addition of satu\u0002rated ferrous sulfate solution) were produced.\nL113@P1: Formate and fumarate did not affect growth or\nL114@P1: hydrogen or H,S production. The reactions ob\u0002served in these media were unaffected by the\nL115@P1: addition of 20 mM MgSO, as compared with\nL116@P1: 0.07 mM MgSO, in the usual media.\nL117@P1: The higher level of MgSO, did affect the reac\u0002tions of all three strains tested in pyruvate\nL118@P1: medium. Pyruvate utilization increased from\nL119@P1: -\nL120@P1: FIG. 1. Desulfomonas zyxwpigra ATCC 29098. Top,\nL121@P1: Cells from PY broth culture. Bottom, Cells from PY\u0002glucose broth culture. See Fig. 11 for scale.\nL122@P1: an average of 53 to 80%, while hydrogen pro\u0002duction decreased from over 3% to less than\nL123@P1: 0.3% in the head space gas. Acetate production\nL124@P1: increased about fourfold. Probable increases in\nL125@P1: cell yield and H,S production were obscured\nL126@P1: because they were very high in both media. The\nL127@P1: high level of MgSO, improved lactate utiliza\u0002tion and markedly decreased hydrogen produc\u0002tion from lactate by the type strain, but little or\nL128@P1: no effect was demonstrated in lactate with the\nL129@P1: other two strains tested. These data indicate\nL130@P1: that the organism reduces sulfate but produces\nL131@P1: hydrogen in its absence, that high levels of H,S\nL132@P1: are produced from peptone and/or cysteine and\nL133@P1: from sulfate, and that energy metabolism is\nL134@P1: inhibited in the absence of sulfate as a hydro\u0002gen acceptor.\nL135@P1: The presence of cytochrome in cells of the\nL136@P1: type strain was demonstrated by J. F. Sperry\nL137@P1: by comparing the absorption spectra of dithio\u0002nate-reduced samples of broken, washed cells\nL138@P1: against air-oxidized samples. Absorption max\u0002ima were at 425, 527, and 556 nm and a mini\u0002mum was at 450 nm. Addition of 2 N NaOH to a\nL139@P1: cellular pellet showed a red fluorescence, indi\u0002cating the presence of desulfoviridin, as re\u0002ported in other sulfate-reducing bacteria.\nL140@P1: Fermentation products. Fermentation prod\u0002ucts (average milliequivalents per 100 ml of\nL141@P1: culture with 0.07 mM MgSO,) were as follows.\nL142@P1: From PY-pyruvate: Acetate (6.0), sometimes\nL143@P1: with trace to moderate amounts of lactate, suc\u0002cinate, or formate. Copious hydrogen was pro\u0002duced.\nL144@P1: From PY or PY-glucose: Acetic acid (1.0).\nL145@P1: From PY-fructose cultures: Small amounts of\nL146@P1: hydrogen, presumably from the PY, were de\u0002tected.\nL147@P1: G +C content zyxwof the DNA. The G+ C content\nL148@P1: is 67 mol% by T,,, for the type strain and 66\nL149@P1-2: mol% for one other strain (A2-24D) tested. TABLE: 1. zyxwvutsrqponmlkj\nL150@P2: 1ieactioii.s zyxwvutsrqpon\nL151@P2: of zyxwvutsrqponmlkj\nL152@P2: LA>sulfhtnoiias pzgra, zyxwvutsrqpon\nL153@P2: Bz/tyrivibrio crossvtus, arid three New species of Ki~rninococcus~ zyxw\nL154@P2: h3 zy\nL155@P2: lb 0 Substrate\nL156@P2: or\nL157@P2: reaction zyxwvutsrqponm\nL158@P2: Am ygdalin\nL159@P2: Arabinose Cellobiose\nL160@P2: Esculin Esculin hydrolysis\nL161@P2: Fructose\nL162@P2: Glucose Glycogen\nL163@P2: Inositol Lactose\nL164@P2: Maltose Mannitol Mannose Melibiose\nL165@P2: Kaffinose Rhamnose Ribose\nL166@P2: Salicin Sorbitol Starch pH Starch hydrolysis\nL167@P2: Sucrose\nL168@P2: Tre halose\nL169@P2: Xylose Milk\nL170@P2: H2S production\nL171@P2: Motility Gas (glucose agar)\nL172@P2: Growth: PY\nL173@P2: Best growth: CHOd\nL174@P2: Growth : gl u c ose-b i le\nL175@P2: Products detected\nL176@P2: G + C content\nL177@P2: UP s zyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfed\nL178@P2: u lj'o t zyxwvutsrqponmlkjihg\nL179@P2: ti o ti as p igm\nL180@P2: ATCC\nL181@P2: 29098\nL182@P2: 1 lb\nL183@P2: 919\"\nL184@P2: Bu tyrivibrio\nL185@P2: CYO sso t us\nL186@P2: 30\nL187@P2: 717\nL188@P2: -\nL189@P2: -+\nL190@P2: W\u0002W\u0002Aw A\u0002A\nL191@P2: Aw\nL192@P2: + C-\nL193@P2: -\nL194@P2: +-\nL195@P2: -\nL196@P2: 19-\nL197@P2: 4\nL198@P2: 1,-\nL199@P2: FLba\nL200@P2: (p ,py ,s)\nL201@P2: K. piavus\nL202@P2: ATCC\nL203@P2: 29149\nL204@P2: A\nL205@P2: A\nL206@P2: -\nL207@P2: W\nL208@P2: +\nL209@P2: A A\nL210@P2: - - -\nL211@P2: A\nL212@P2: -\nL213@P2: -\nL214@P2: W\nL215@P2: A A A A\nL216@P2: -\nL217@P2: W\nL218@P2: + A - A\nL219@P2: -\nL220@P2: + -\nL221@P2: 4\nL222@P2: 1\nL223@P2: 4 4\nL224@P2: FA12\nL225@P2: 43\nL226@P2: 22\nL227@P2: 11/6\nL228@P2: -A\nL229@P2: A\nL230@P2: -\nL231@P2: W\nL232@P2: +\nL233@P2: A A\nL234@P2: -\nL235@P2: -\nL236@P2: -A\nL237@P2: A\nL238@P2: - -\nL239@P2: wA\nL240@P2: A\nL241@P2: A\nL242@P2: A A\nL243@P2: - -A\nL244@P2: +-\nL245@P2: A-\nL246@P2: -\nL247@P2: A\nL248@P2: C\u0002V\nL249@P2: -+\nL250@P2: 42\nL251@P2: 192\nL252@P2: 4\nL253@P2: 472\nL254@P2: AF12\nL255@P2: (S,PY)\nL256@P2: --\nL257@P2: K ulninococcus\nL258@P2: K. lacturis K. obeurn\nL259@P2: 46 % 21/14 M ATCC 29174\nL260@P2: -\nL261@P2: A A\nL262@P2: -\nL263@P2: +\nL264@P2: A A\nL265@P2: - -\nL266@P2: A A\nL267@P2: -\nL268@P2: A A A\nL269@P2: A\nL270@P2: A\nL271@P2: -\nL272@P2: A W\nL273@P2: -\nL274@P2: A\nL275@P2: -\nL276@P2: A\nL277@P2: C -I-\nL278@P2: -\nL279@P2: 2\nL280@P2: 1\nL281@P2: 4\nL282@P2: 3\nL283@P2: A2\nL284@P2: 41\nL285@P2: 8\nL286@P2: X\nL287@P2: A 1:\nL288@P2: \"Z\nL289@P2: -A\nL290@P2: A\nL291@P2: -\nL292@P2: +-\nL293@P2: A A s\nL294@P2: 51\nL295@P2: - 3\nL296@P2: s\nL297@P2: 5\nL298@P2: -\nL299@P2: A A\nL300@P2: -A\nL301@P2: A\nL302@P2: A A\nL303@P2: A\nL304@P2: A\nL305@P2: -A\nL306@P2: A\u0002z\nL307@P2: -W\nL308@P2: -\nL309@P2: A\nL310@P2: -A\nL311@P2: A\nL312@P2: C\nL313@P2: +-\nL314@P2: - 2,4 F\nL315@P2: %\nL316@P2: A2 5\nL317@P2: (1s)\nL318@P2: !+\nL319@P2: 4\nL320@P2: w\nL321@P2: 1 I\u00024\nL322@P2: V\nL323@P2-3: See legend, p. 241. VOL. 26, zyxwvutsr\nL324@P3: 1976 EMENDATION OF BACTEROIDACEAE zyxw\nL325@P3: 241\nL326@P3: Type strain. zyxwThe type strain is American\nL327@P3: Type Culture Collection (ATCC) 29098 zy\nL328@P3: (= Vir\u0002ginia Polytechnic Institute [VPI] C3-23); iso\u0002lated from human feces.\nL329@P3: Comments. This group was described previ\u0002ously (9) as Bacteroides L-3.\nL330@P3: Strains placed in this species are: A2-24D,\nL331@P3: A18-14, B6-30C, C3-23, C32-31, and S9-39 from\nL332@P3: feces; M3-381 from the contents of a transverse\nL333@P3: colon; and 9021 from a ruptured sigmoid colon.\nL334@P3: Other isolates were from similar sites. With\nL335@P3: routine tests zyxw\nL336@P3: D. pigra superficially appears to\nL337@P3: be similar to B. corrodens and other anaerobic\nL338@P3: nonfermentative, gram-negative rods. How\u0002ever, it differs from them by not reducing ni\u0002trate, not producing oxidase, and by producing\nL339@P3: H,S and converting pyruvate to large amounts\nL340@P3: of acetate and hydrogen. Hydrogen production\nL341@P3: is decreased in the presence of large amounts\nL342@P3: (20 mM) of MgSO,. The G+C content of the\nL343@P3: DNA of D. pigra is 66 to 67 mol%, whereas that\nL344@P3: of B. corrodens is 28 to 30 mol% (6).\nL345@P3: This species of anaerobic, nonsporeforming,\nL346@P3: gram-negative, nonmotile, sulfate-reducing\nL347@P3: rods is placed in the new genus Desulfomonas\nL348@P3: rather than the existing sulfate-reducing ge\u0002nus Desulfotomaculum or Desulfovibrio be\u0002cause spores could not be detected in basal me\u0002dium or in glucose or pyruvate cultures by\nL349@P3: spore stain or by survival of cultures at 80 C for\nL350@P3: 10 min, 70 C for 10 min, or in 70% alcohol for 1\nL351@P3: h. Neither motility nor flagella could be demon\u0002strated in any cultures.\nL352@P3: Emendation of Butyriuibrio; B. crossotus\nL353@P3: sp. nov. The genus Butyriuibrio currently in\u0002cludes the single species B. fibrisolvens, and\nL354@P3: the genus is described on the basis of this mono\u0002trichous species. During our studies of the in\u0002testinal bacteria, one group was detected which\nL355@P3: is similar to B. fibrisolvens in that the orga\u0002nisms are anaerobic, gram-negative, curved\nL356@P3: rods that are saccharolytic and grow poorly or\nL357@P3: not at all without a fermentable carbohydrate,\nL358@P3: from which formic, butyric, lactic, and acetic\nL359@P3: acids are produced. These strains, however, are\nL360@P3: lophotrichous (Fig. 21, unlike strains ofB. fibri\u0002soluens, which either are monotrichous or occa\u0002sionally have two polar flagella. Although other\nL361@P3: individual properties distinguish the new spe\u0002cies from B. fibrisolvens, we believe that the\nL362@P3: major characteristics are so similar as to place\nL363@P3: the organism in the same genus with B. fibri\u0002soluens. We do not believe that the number of\nL364@P3: polar flagella is a sufficiently important charac\u0002teristic to warrant the creation of a new genus,\nL365@P3: particularly since individual strains of bacteria\nL366@P3: with polar flagella vary in respect to numbers\nL367@P3-4: of flagella. Therefore, we propose an emended 242 MOORE, JOHNSON, AND HOLDEMAN zyxwvu\nL368@P4: INT. J. SYST. BACTERIOL.\nL369@P4: photrichous cells. See\nL370@P4: FIG. 2. zyxwvutsrqpoButyrivibrio\nL371@P4: Fig.\nL372@P4: crossotus\nL373@P4: ll for scale.\nL374@P4: ATCC 291 75. Lo- zyxwvut\nL375@P4: description of the genus Butyrivibrio to include\nL376@P4: curved rods which occur singly or in* chains or\nL377@P4: filaments and which may or may not be helical.\nL378@P4: Cells are motile, monotrichous or lophotri\u0002chous, with polar or subpolar flagella; nonmo\u0002tile variants may occur. No resting stages are\nL379@P4: known. Cells are gram negative. Metabolism is\nL380@P4: fermentative, carbohydrates being the main\nL381@P4: fermentable substrates, with the production of\nL382@P4: butyrate as one of the important products.\nL383@P4: Strains are strictly anaerobic.\nL384@P4: Butyrivibrio crossotus sp. nov. (Gr. adj.\nL385@P4: cross. so’tus tasseled [referring to the multiple\nL386@P4: polar or subpolar flagella]). Cells are obligately\nL387@P4: anaerobic, lophotrichous (polar or subpolar),\nL388@P4: nonsporeforming, gram-negative rods with\nL389@P4: rounded to slightly tapered ends. Cells were\nL390@P4: usually curved, at least soon after isolation\nL391@P4: from the natural habitat, and occurred singly\nL392@P4: or in pairs and short chains (Fig. 2 and 3). Pairs\nL393@P4: were often in an “S” arrangement. From PYG\nL394@P4: broth cultures, cells were 0.5 to 0.7 pm wide\nL395@P4: and 2.0 to 5.0 pm long. Three of 29 isolates\nL396@P4: tested were nonmotile, and no flagella could be\nL397@P4: seen in flagella stains from several subcultures;\nL398@P4: however, they had the other characteristics\nL399@P4: typical for this species, and they are probably\nL400@P4: nonmotile variants. However, it is also possible\nL401@P4: that the flagella were broken off during prepa\u0002ration of the suspension for flagella staining.\nL402@P4: Colonies. After incubation for 5 days in\nL403@P4: RGCA roll tubes, subsurface colonies were 0.5\nL404@P4: to 1.0 mm in diameter, lenticular, and translu\u0002cent\nL405@P4: S\nL406@P4: circular,\nL407@P4: roll\nL408@P4: to\nL409@P4: streaks\nL410@P4: transparent.\nL411@P4: entire,\nL412@P4: were\nL413@P4: convex,\nL414@P4: 0.2\nL415@P4: Surface\nL416@P4: to\nL417@P4: translucent\nL418@P4: 1.0\nL419@P4: colonies\nL420@P4: mm in diameter,\nL421@P4: on\nL422@P4: to\nL423@P4: BHIA\u0002semi- zyxwv\nL424@P4: opaque, and smooth. Only 3 of the 10 strains\nL425@P4: tested grew as surface colonies on BAP incu\u0002bated anaerobically; surface colonies were the\nL426@P4: same as on the BHIA-S roll streaks, and no\nL427@P4: hemolytic activity was observed. The few\nL428@P4: strains that grew on EYA produced no lecithin\u0002ase or lipase reaction.\nL429@P4: Cultural characteristic‘s. There was poor\nL430@P4: growth in PY broth that did not contain a fer\u0002mentable carbohydrate. PY-maltose cultures\nL431@P4: had abundant growth with smooth, flocculent,\nL432@P4: or ropy sediment; there usually was some tur\u0002bidity.\nL433@P4: The optimum temperature for growth was\nL434@P4: 37 C. Some strains grew equally well at 45 C.\nL435@P4: There was either no growth or very poor growth\nL436@P4: at 30 C.\nL437@P4: Biochemical reactions. Some of the biochemi\u0002cal characteristics of 31 isolates of this species\nL438@P4: are given in Table 1. In addition, the type\nL439@P4: strain and nine other strains tested did not\nL440@P4: grow well in, or ferment, adonitol, galactose,\nL441@P4: glycerol, inulin, or sorbose. All produced acid in\nL442@P4: PY-dextrin. There was little or no growth in PY\nL443@P4: media containing lactate, pyruvate, or threo\u0002nine. No ammonia was detected from PY or CM\nL444@P4: cultures (with Nessler reagent).\nL445@P4: After incubation for 5 days, the pH was 4.7 to\nL446@P4: 5.2 in the PY-maltose cultures.\nL447@P4: Fermentation products. Fermentation prod\u0002ucts (average milliequivalents per 100 ml of\nL448@P4: culture) were as follows.\nL449@P4: From PY-maltose: Formic (2.0), zy\nL450@P4: